namespace fast {
/** @page python-tutorial-intro Python Introduction Tutorial
@tableofcontents

This is an introduction tutorial for FAST in Python.
To follow this Python FAST tutorial, please [install FAST for Python](@ref install-python) first,
and verify that your installation is working.

Download test data
-----------------------------
To download the ~2 GB FAST test dataset, simply start your Python interpreter
and execute the following Python code:

```py
import fast
fast.downloadTestDataIfNotExists()
```

Import image data
-----------------------------
We will start by importing an ultrasound image from disk stored in the metaimage (mhd) format:

```py
import fast
importer = fast.ImageFileImporter(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_0.mhd')
```

This code will create an ImageFileImporter process object and set the filename of the ultrasound image to load.
This importer can load images stored in many different formats such as MHD, Dicom, JPEG, PNG, BMP.
Config::getTestDataPath simply returns the path to where the test data you downloaded earlier is located.
Note that this code only creates the importer object, it has not loaded the image yet.
To execute the ImageFileImporter, and thereby loading the image, you can do the following:

```py
image = importer.run().getOutput()
print("An image was imported with size: ", image.getWidth(), image.getHeight())
```

Calling run() and getOutput() can be omitted by just using the call operator () of the importer object:
```py
image = importer()
print("An image was imported with size: ", image.getWidth(), image.getHeight())
```

This can then be reduced to a one line operation (note the () at the end):
```py
import fast
image = fast.ImageFileImporter(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_0.mhd')()
print("An image was imported with size: ", image.getWidth(), image.getHeight())
```

Display the image data
-----------------------------
To display the image data, we need two things: a Window and a Renderer.
For this example, we will use the ImageRenderer which is used to display images, and the SimpleWindow in 2D mode.
The renderer is connected to the importer using the connect method:

@code{.py}
renderer = fast.ImageRenderer().connect(importer)
@endcode

This will form an execution pipeline, so that executing the renderer will
trigger execute on the importer as well.

Our entire example should now look like this:

@code{.py}
import fast
importer = fast.ImageFileImporter(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_0.mhd')

# Set up a renderer and connect it to the importer
renderer = fast.ImageRenderer().connect(importer)

# Create a window, add the renderer and start the computation/rendering loop.
fast.SimpleWindow2D().connect(renderer).run()
@endcode

You should now see this image:
@image html images/examples/python/left_ventricle.jpg

@par Execution pipeline
    Each process object in FAST has zero or more input and outputs ports.
    Process objects are connected together using the connect method thus forming
    a pipeline, or a computational graph if you will.
    The process objects are not executed until run() or runAndGetOutputData() is called.
    The run call will recursively call run on all its parent process objects. A process object will execute if
    it has new/updated input data or if a parameter has changed i.e. it is marked as being modified.
    In the example above, the computation/rendering loop started by the window will call run() repeatedly on the renderer,
    thereby triggering execution of the entire pipeline.
    [Read more about the concepts and glossary used in FAST here.](@ref concepts)

Add an image processing step
-----------------------------

We will now add an image processing step to our pipeline.
For this, we will use the NonLocalMeans filter which is excellent at
de-noising ultrasound images.

@code{.py}
import fast
importer = fast.ImageFileImporter(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_0.mhd')

# Set up the NonLocalMeans processing step and connect it to the importer
nlm = fast.NonLocalMeans().connect(importer)

# Set up a renderer and connect it to the NLM filter
renderer = fast.ImageRenderer().connect(nlm)

# Create a window, add the renderer and start the computation/rendering loop.
fast.SimpleWindow2D()\
    .connect(renderer)\
    .run()
@endcode

The results should look like this:
@image html images/tutorials/left_ventricle_non_local_means.jpg

Dual view window
-----------------------------
Now, let's say we want to display the input image and the filtered image side-by-side.
The SimpleWindow used above only has a single View, to show two images side-by-side we will need two View objects and
therefore we will use the DualViewWindow instead. We also need an additional ImageRenderer which is connected to the ImageFileImporter.
Putting this all together, it should look like this:
@code{.py}
import fast
importer = fast.ImageFileImporter(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_0.mhd')

# Set up the NonLocalMeans processing step and connect it to the importer
nlm = fast.NonLocalMeans().connect(importer)

# Set a renderer and connect it to importer
renderer = fast.ImageRenderer().connect(importer)

# Set a renderer and connect it to the NonLocalMeans filter
filterRenderer = fast.ImageRenderer().connect(nlm)

# Create a dual view window, add the renderers and start the computation/rendering loop.
fast.DualViewWindow2D()\
    .connectLeft(renderer)\
    .connectRight(filterRenderer)\
    .run()
@endcode

The result should look like this:
@image html images/tutorials/left_ventricle_nlm_dual_view.jpg


Data streaming
-----------------------------
It might seem cumbersome and counterintuitive to create a processing pipeline with connect() before actually executing the code with run().
However, this has one major advantage which is that the initialization and execution steps are separated.
A processing step may have a high initialization cost. Such initialization may for instance be creating some lookup table, compiling kernels, loading something from disk etc.
When we want to process many data elements, we only want to perform this initialization once.
Thus, efficient processing of data streams becomes very easy with FAST.

For example, to process an ultrasound recording, instead of just one single image, the only thing you need to
change in the previous example, is to replace the ImageFileImporter with an ImageFileStreamer:

@code{.py}
import fast

# Stream image frames of an ultrasound recording, this will create its own thread
importer = fast.ImageFileStreamer(
    fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + '/US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_#.mhd',
    loop=True,
    framerate=10,
)

nlm = fast.NonLocalMeans().connect(importer)

renderer = fast.ImageRenderer().connect(nlm)

# Setup window; this will start a processing loop in its own thread,
# and start a rendering/GUI loop in the main thread.
fast.SimpleWindow2D()\
    .connect(renderer)\
    .run()
@endcode

This will process an entire ultrasound recording through the same pipeline as before, without the need to create a loop or manually updating the visualization.
FAST creates a processing loop which runs in its own thread.
Rendering and GUI logic happens in a separate thread.
This is all setup by FAST in the Window objects.
In addition, most streamers in FAST, create their own thread for streaming the data.
Thus processing, visualization and data loading all happen in parallel in their own threads.

@par Streamers
    If you want to try streaming from your web camera, try out the CameraStreamer.
    If you want to try streaming from a video check out the MovieStreamer, but this requires you to install video codecs.

If you want to iterate through a stream of data manually, you can do this with a simple loop as follows:
@code{.py}
import fast
streamer = fast.ImageFileStreamer(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + '/US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_#.mhd')

nlm = fast.NonLocalMeans().connect(streamer)

# Iterate through all frames in data stream
frameNr = 0
for image in fast.DataStream(nlm):
    print('Received image frame', frameNr, 'with size', image.getWidth(), image.getHeight())
    frameNr += 1
@endcode

Image segmentation
-----------------------------------------

Segmentation is the process of classifying each pixel in a image.
In FAST, a segmentation is represented as an Image object with 8-bit unsigned integer data type and a single channel.
Typically, segmentations are displayed as transparent color overlays on the original image.
In the example below, a simple binary thresholding is applied to a stream of ultrasound images.
A SegmentationRenderer is used to render a transparent overlay on the input image which is, as before, rendered
using an ImageRenderer:

@code{.py}
import fast

streamer = fast.ImageFileStreamer(
    fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + '/US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_#.mhd',
    loop=True,
    framerate=10
)
segmentation = fast.BinaryThresholding(100).connect(streamer)

# Create an image renderer and a segmentation renderer
imageRenderer = fast.ImageRenderer().connect(streamer)
segmentationRenderer = fast.SegmentationRenderer().connect(segmentation)

# Connect renderers to window and run the whole pipeline
fast.SimpleWindow2D(bgcolor=fast.Color.Black())\
    .connect([imageRenderer, segmentationRenderer])\
    .run()
@endcode

To reduce boilerplate code you can use the [display2D and display3D](@ref shortcuts) functions for displaying data and results.
The example below is equivalent to the one above, but with only 1 line of code for the data display part.

@code{.py}
import fast

streamer = fast.ImageFileStreamer(
    fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + '/US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_#.mhd',
    loop=True,
    framerate=10
)
segmentation = fast.BinaryThresholding(100).connect(streamer)

# Reduce boilerplate code with setting up renderers and window by using the display2D function:
fast.display2D(streamer, segmentation, bgcolor=fast.Color.Black())
@endcode

Integration with other Python libraries
-----------------------------------------
Images in FAST implements the [Python array interface](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/arrays.interface.html), and may thus be passed onto other Python libraries which
can read this array interface, e.g. NumPy and Matplotlib.

### Convert FAST image to NumPy ndarray

```py
import fast
import numpy as np

# Import an image to FAST
fast_image = fast.ImageFileImporter(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_0.mhd')()

# Convert the FAST image to a NumPy ndarray
numpy_image = np.asarray(fast_image)
```

### Create FAST image from a NumPy ndarray

```py
import fast
import numpy as np
from PIL import Image

image = Image.open(fast.Config.getDocumentationPath() + 'images/FAST_logo_square.png')
numpy_image = np.asarray(image)

# Create FAST image from the NumPy ndarray
fast_image = fast.Image.createFromArray(numpy_image)
```

### Create FAST image from PyTorch tensor

Use the fast.Image.createFromTensor method to convert a PyTorch tensor to a FAST Image:
```py
import fast
import torchvision

# Read image with torchvision to a torch.Tensor:
image_tensor = torchvision.io.read_image(fast.Config.getDocumentationPath() + 'images/FAST_logo_square.png')

# Convert torch Tensor to fast Image using createFromTensor:
fast_image = fast.Image.createFromTensor(image_tensor)
```

### Convert FAST Image to PyTorch tensor

Use numpy asarray method to convert a FAST image to a PyTorch tensor.
Note that FAST uses 'channel last' image dimension ordering, while PyTorch uses 'channel first' image dimension ordering.
Therefore you need to permute the image tensor.
```py
import fast
import torch
import numpy as np

# Import an image to FAST
fast_image = fast.ImageFileImporter(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_0.mhd')()

tensor = torch.tensor(np.asarray(fast_image)).permute(2, 0, 1)
```

### Visualize FAST image with Matplotlib

```py
import fast
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np

fast_image = fast.ImageFileImporter(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_0.mhd')()
numpy_image = np.asarray(fast_image)

plt.imshow(numpy_image[..., 0], cmap='gray')
plt.show()
```

### Create FAST image from an OpenCV image

Like with numpy, and OpenCV image can easily be converted to a FAST image using the createFromArray method:
```py
import fast
import cv2

# Load image from disk with OpenCV
cv_image = cv2.imread(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + "US/US-2D.jpg")

# Convert OpenCV image to FAST
fast_image = fast.Image.createFromArray(cv_image)

# Visualize with FAST
fast.display2D(fast_image)
```

### Create OpenCV image from a FAST image

An OpenCV image in Python is basically just a NumPy array, therefore you can use the np.asarray function
to convert a FAST image to an OpenCV image.

```py
import fast
import cv2
import numpy as np

# Import image with FAST
fast_image = fast.ImageFileImporter(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + "US/US-2D.jpg")()

# Convert FAST image to a NumPy array/OpenCV image
cv_image = np.asarray(fast_image)

# Visualize the image with OpenCV
cv2.imshow('FAST image!', cv_image)
cv2.waitKey(1000*3) # Display for 3 seconds
cv2.destroyAllWindows()
```

### Use Python code (NumPy, scikit-image, OpenCV ++) in a FAST pipeline

You can inject any Python code into a FAST processing pipeline by extending the PythonProcessObject class.
Here is a complete example on how to do this:

@include Python/python_process_object.py

## Run OpenCL code from Python

FAST provides a high-level interface for using OpenCL in process objects in Python.
[See the GPU processing in Python tutorial for more information](@ref python-tutorial-opencl).

Exporting data
---------------------
To save data in a given file format, you can use any of the [FAST Exporters](@ref exporters).
For instance to save an image as a jpeg file you can use the ImageFileExporter.
ImageFileExporter will recognize the filename extension, e.g. '.jpg', and select the correct exporting routine.

@code{.py}
fast.ImageFileExporter('filename.jpg').connect(image).run()
@endcode

This can further simplified by replacing connect() and run() with the call operator () like so:

@code{.py}
fast.ImageFileExporter('filename.jpg')(image)
@endcode

Differences between using FAST in C++ and Python
-------------------------------------------
Note that the API documentation on this page is generated from the C++ code and there are some differences to be aware of.
While in C++ the FAST process and data objects have to be created using the static create() methods, in Python you
can create the these objects with what looks like the constructor (although it is actually the call operator of a metaclass, which then calls the create() method).
Thus with pyFAST `SomeObject.create(parameter=value)` and `SomeObject(parameter=value)` are equivalent.
For these objects in the documentation you will only see the create() and not the constructor/call operator definitions, but the arguments are generally the same and can be used interchangeably.

Next steps
---------------------

You have now finished the Python introduction tutorial to FAST.

